1
00:00:02,323 --> 00:00:03,534
Hello everyone.

2
00:00:03,534 --> 00:00:09,427
We are Team G2, and this is our Data Science complex engineering problem:

3
00:00:09,427 --> 00:00:13,625
Smart Traffic Signal Optimization using Game Theory.

4
00:00:13,625 --> 00:00:22,828
It supports Sustainable Development Goal eleven, sustainable cities, in particular safe and sustainable transport.

5
00:00:22,828 --> 00:00:30,013
In this video I will explain the idea, show the live system, and show what the data says,

6
00:00:30,013 --> 00:00:32,515
including what it does not say.

7
00:00:32,977 --> 00:00:35,351
Let us start with the problem.

8
00:00:35,351 --> 00:00:44,449
Most traffic signals run on a fixed timer: twenty seconds for one road, twenty for the other, whatever the traffic.

9
00:00:44,911 --> 00:00:52,323
The timer cannot see that one road is empty, so cars wait at red in front of an empty cross street.

10
00:00:52,323 --> 00:01:00,558
And a signal that only looks at its own queue can push cars into a neighbouring junction that is already full,

11
00:01:00,558 --> 00:01:03,253
which then blocks that junction too.

12
00:01:03,253 --> 00:01:04,975
We call this spillback.

13
00:01:05,438 --> 00:01:09,567
And an ambulance has no way to ask for priority.

14
00:01:09,567 --> 00:01:13,094
It waits in the queue like everyone else.

15
00:01:13,553 --> 00:01:14,971
Here is our idea.

16
00:01:14,971 --> 00:01:18,559
Treat every junction as a player in a game.

17
00:01:18,559 --> 00:01:26,067
Each signal controls only its own phase, but its result depends on what its neighbours do.

18
00:01:26,067 --> 00:01:30,739
That is exactly the structure of a non-cooperative game.

19
00:01:30,739 --> 00:01:38,081
The players are the junctions: four on a two by two grid, nine on a three by three grid.

20
00:01:38,532 --> 00:01:45,636
Each player has two strategies: give the next green to North-South, or to East-West.

21
00:01:46,092 --> 00:01:49,313
Each player has a payoff, which is a score.

22
00:01:49,313 --> 00:01:54,857
It gains for every vehicle it can clear, and for waiting time it relieves.

23
00:01:54,857 --> 00:01:59,577
It loses a little for switching, because switching wastes time.

24
00:01:59,577 --> 00:02:07,742
It loses for pushing cars into a neighbour whose road is already full; that is the spillback penalty, lambda.

25
00:02:07,742 --> 00:02:15,084
And it gains a bonus for matching a neighbour that is sending it a platoon of cars; that is kappa.

26
00:02:15,545 --> 00:02:18,894
The solution concept is a Nash equilibrium:

27
00:02:18,894 --> 00:02:25,204
a set of choices where no junction can do better by changing only its own choice.

28
00:02:25,204 --> 00:02:29,489
That is stable, because nobody has a reason to deviate.

29
00:02:29,949 --> 00:02:33,277
How do we find that equilibrium every second?

30
00:02:33,277 --> 00:02:39,932
Each junction reads its detectors: how many vehicles are queued, how many are approaching,

31
00:02:39,932 --> 00:02:45,477
how long they have waited, and whether an ambulance or a blockage is there.

32
00:02:45,928 --> 00:02:52,053
Then, one after another, each junction picks the phase with the higher payoff,

33
00:02:52,053 --> 00:02:55,352
given what the others are currently doing.

34
00:02:55,352 --> 00:02:58,336
This is called best-response dynamics.

35
00:02:58,788 --> 00:03:02,515
We repeat until a full round changes nothing.

36
00:03:02,515 --> 00:03:05,165
That is a pure Nash equilibrium.

37
00:03:05,165 --> 00:03:12,204
In our experiments this happened in every single decision, usually within two rounds.

38
00:03:12,656 --> 00:03:21,001
Then a safety layer applies: yellow two and a half seconds, all-red one second, minimum green eight seconds,

39
00:03:21,001 --> 00:03:22,469
maximum forty-five.

40
00:03:22,469 --> 00:03:25,096
Conflicting greens are impossible.

41
00:03:25,096 --> 00:03:30,814
We compare against two baselines: a fixed timer, and a selfish controller,

42
00:03:30,814 --> 00:03:35,528
which is the same game with the neighbour terms switched off.

43
00:03:35,978 --> 00:03:37,521
Now the live system.

44
00:03:37,521 --> 00:03:44,618
This is a traffic simulator with cars, bikes, auto-rickshaws and buses, driving on the left.

45
00:03:44,618 --> 00:03:47,317
The traffic lights are the players.

46
00:03:47,317 --> 00:03:52,331
The red tint shows queues, and the blue band is a green corridor,

47
00:03:52,331 --> 00:03:56,882
where two neighbouring junctions are serving the same road.

48
00:03:57,334 --> 00:04:05,831
Below the map, the three controllers run side by side in the background, on exactly the same vehicle arrivals,

49
00:04:05,831 --> 00:04:07,839
so the comparison is fair.

50
00:04:07,839 --> 00:04:10,697
The map shows whichever one I select.

51
00:04:10,697 --> 00:04:19,966
After a minute or two you can see the fixed timer in grey has the highest delay, and the adaptive controllers are lower.

52
00:04:20,426 --> 00:04:28,255
The tiles show average delay, vehicles stopped, throughput, and how often the equilibrium was reached.

53
00:04:28,255 --> 00:04:35,546
Idling time and a carbon dioxide figure are there too, but that figure is only an illustration.

54
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:40,204
The signal timeline shows every junction's phases.

55
00:04:40,204 --> 00:04:43,819
Here is the fixed timer: a regular pattern.

56
00:04:43,819 --> 00:04:50,208
Now the Nash controller: the greens stretch and shrink to follow the queues.

57
00:04:50,669 --> 00:04:57,599
Clicking a junction opens the inspector: the queue on every approach, the payoff of each action,

58
00:04:57,599 --> 00:04:59,909
and the reason for the decision.

59
00:05:00,365 --> 00:05:04,098
The Game model tab shows the game itself, live.

60
00:05:04,098 --> 00:05:11,405
Here is the payoff formula, and sliders to tune the weights while the simulation is running.

61
00:05:11,869 --> 00:05:16,443
This is the payoff matrix for two neighbouring junctions.

62
00:05:16,443 --> 00:05:18,770
Each cell shows both payoffs.

63
00:05:18,770 --> 00:05:24,628
The underlined number is the best response to what the other player does.

64
00:05:24,628 --> 00:05:30,085
The shaded cell, where both are underlined, is the Nash equilibrium.

65
00:05:30,540 --> 00:05:38,484
Below it are the best-response rounds: junctions take turns, and by the second round nobody wants to change.

66
00:05:38,940 --> 00:05:42,517
New in version two is the equilibrium analysis.

67
00:05:42,517 --> 00:05:48,680
Every second the simulator lists all the joint plans, sixteen for four junctions,

68
00:05:48,680 --> 00:05:54,996
checks which are Nash equilibria, and finds the plan with the highest total payoff.

69
00:05:54,996 --> 00:05:59,028
The highlighted row is the plan the controller chose.

70
00:05:59,487 --> 00:06:06,919
Over ten seeds, the dynamics reached an equilibrium every time, there was almost always exactly one,

71
00:06:06,919 --> 00:06:13,385
and it captured ninety-seven to ninety-eight percent of the best possible joint payoff.

72
00:06:13,385 --> 00:06:19,107
It was the very best plan in fifty-seven to eighty-four percent of decisions.

73
00:06:19,107 --> 00:06:25,647
That gap is the price of anarchy of this game, and we measure it instead of assuming it.

74
00:06:26,105 --> 00:06:28,164
Now let us make it dynamic.

75
00:06:28,164 --> 00:06:31,822
I go back to the simulation, select junction J2,

76
00:06:31,822 --> 00:06:39,521
and block its approach from the west for a hundred and twenty seconds, like an accident or road work.

77
00:06:39,980 --> 00:06:44,984
The striped barrier on the map is the blockage, with a countdown.

78
00:06:44,984 --> 00:06:52,451
The adaptive controllers see it through their detectors, so they stop giving that approach green,

79
00:06:52,451 --> 00:06:55,915
and the upstream junctions hold traffic back.

80
00:06:55,915 --> 00:07:02,536
The fixed timer cannot see it, and keeps giving green to a stop line nobody can cross.

81
00:07:02,536 --> 00:07:04,460
The event log records it.

82
00:07:04,921 --> 00:07:06,518
Next, an ambulance.

83
00:07:06,518 --> 00:07:09,544
I send one from the west on row one.

84
00:07:09,544 --> 00:07:12,737
It has flashing lights and a red halo.

85
00:07:12,737 --> 00:07:19,041
Under preemption, a large priority term is added to the payoff of its road,

86
00:07:19,041 --> 00:07:22,907
and the minimum green is cut to three seconds.

87
00:07:22,907 --> 00:07:27,529
Yellow and all-red are never skipped, so it stays safe.

88
00:07:27,990 --> 00:07:32,942
The event log shows junctions pre-empting for the ambulance.

89
00:07:32,942 --> 00:07:39,462
One run is noisy, so I will send a few more, on different roads, to get a mean.

90
00:07:51,147 --> 00:07:56,430
The panel shows the latest and the mean trip time for each controller.

91
00:07:56,430 --> 00:08:00,052
Fixed-time is slower, because it cannot respond.

92
00:08:00,052 --> 00:08:03,523
Now I switch preemption off and send one more.

93
00:08:03,523 --> 00:08:09,334
A single run is noisy, so the fair comparison is the average over many seeds,

94
00:08:09,334 --> 00:08:14,692
which we will see in the benchmark: about fifty seconds for fixed-time,

95
00:08:14,692 --> 00:08:20,955
forty-three for the adaptive controller without preemption, and thirty-two with it.

96
00:08:21,413 --> 00:08:25,234
To make this rigorous, we use the Benchmark tab.

97
00:08:25,234 --> 00:08:32,717
It runs all three controllers on the same traffic, for ten seeds of fifteen simulated minutes.

98
00:08:32,717 --> 00:08:40,200
For each seed, every controller sees identical arrivals; this is called common random numbers.

99
00:08:40,200 --> 00:08:49,037
We inject blocked roads and ambulances, the same events for every controller, and compare with a paired t-test.

100
00:08:49,503 --> 00:08:50,989
Here is the result.

101
00:08:50,989 --> 00:08:57,559
The Nash controller lowers average delay by about a quarter against the fixed timer,

102
00:08:57,559 --> 00:09:03,348
with p below zero point zero zero one, and it shortens the ambulance trip.

103
00:09:03,348 --> 00:09:08,588
Delay counts every vehicle, including those still waiting to enter,

104
00:09:08,588 --> 00:09:13,047
so a controller cannot look good by leaving cars outside.

105
00:09:13,502 --> 00:09:15,801
But look at the last column.

106
00:09:15,801 --> 00:09:22,369
Nash against selfish: the difference is small and not statistically significant.

107
00:09:22,369 --> 00:09:28,526
We will come back to that, because it is the most important honest finding.

108
00:09:28,977 --> 00:09:32,442
The lambda sweep varies the spillback penalty.

109
00:09:32,442 --> 00:09:37,188
It stays flat for small values, and rises when lambda is large,

110
00:09:37,188 --> 00:09:44,193
because the junctions become over-cautious and hold traffic back to protect their neighbours.

111
00:09:44,653 --> 00:09:53,197
Every run is saved in the history, and can be downloaded as CSV or JSON, so the numbers are reproducible.

112
00:09:53,652 --> 00:09:55,835
Across six traffic scenarios,

113
00:09:55,835 --> 00:10:03,288
the adaptive controllers cut average delay by eight to sixty-three percent against the fixed timer,

114
00:10:03,288 --> 00:10:07,730
and every paired test has p below zero point zero zero one.

115
00:10:07,730 --> 00:10:14,129
The gain is largest when one direction is much busier, and smallest under heavy load,

116
00:10:14,129 --> 00:10:17,366
where the whole network is near saturation.

117
00:10:17,366 --> 00:10:23,916
With preemption, the ambulance trip falls from about fifty seconds to about thirty-two.

118
00:10:24,368 --> 00:10:26,312
Now what we did not find.

119
00:10:26,312 --> 00:10:34,402
The coupled Nash game was not significantly better than the selfish adaptive controller in any scenario.

120
00:10:34,402 --> 00:10:38,836
So most of the gain comes from reacting to queues at all.

121
00:10:38,836 --> 00:10:44,047
What the game formulation gives us is an explainable decision rule,

122
00:10:44,047 --> 00:10:52,059
an equilibrium we can verify by enumeration, and a natural way to add emergency priority and incidents.

123
00:10:52,059 --> 00:10:58,904
And all of this is on synthetic demand, in a model not yet calibrated against real data.

124
00:10:59,367 --> 00:11:02,377
This is a complex engineering problem.

125
00:11:02,377 --> 00:11:08,792
It needs game theory, traffic modelling, statistics and web engineering together.

126
00:11:08,792 --> 00:11:14,653
Its requirements conflict: a junction's own benefit against the network's,

127
00:11:14,653 --> 00:11:18,454
ambulance priority against everyone else's wait.

128
00:11:18,454 --> 00:11:27,325
There is no standard solution, the stakeholders have different needs, and the parts are strongly interdependent.

129
00:11:27,325 --> 00:11:33,977
The report maps all of this to the complex problem attributes, W P one to W P seven,

130
00:11:33,977 --> 00:11:37,383
and to Sustainable Development Goal eleven.

131
00:11:37,836 --> 00:11:44,772
To summarise: signals as players in a game, a Nash equilibrium of their payoffs every second,

132
00:11:44,772 --> 00:11:51,858
better than a fixed timer by eight to sixty-three percent in simulation, faster for ambulances,

133
00:11:51,858 --> 00:11:57,974
resilient to blocked roads, and honest about what the data does and does not show.

134
00:11:57,974 --> 00:12:04,165
It is built with JavaScript and the browser canvas, tested with Node, and the code,

135
00:12:04,165 --> 00:12:06,925
report and this script are on GitHub.

136
00:12:06,925 --> 00:12:07,725
Thank you.

137
00:12:07,725 --> 00:12:10,410
We are happy to take your questions.

